  "step_3": "First, comparing the information considered: The decision reason requires assessing skewness, specifically the proportion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes. The individual did not mention anything about the distribution of probabilities across outcomes or the shape of the distribution. They focused on the relative size of the payoff and the relative probability, without referencing the concentration of probability on low or zero outcomes. Second, comparing the decision rule: The reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery (i.e., the one with more probability on unfavorable outcomes), which would be Lottery B (80% on 0). However, the individual justified their choice based on preferring higher probability (25% vs. 20%), which aligns with choosing Lottery A. This contradicts the preference of the decision reason, which would favor Lottery B. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based reason; instead, they used a different heuristic based on probability magnitude. The verbal report shows no evidence of considering skewness as a factor. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
